{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791288779,"nonce":"Ilvi8LVv/DuOYvrkU9oAGmbnUmY=","title":"LLVM: pool allocations have no allocation metadata and internal functions promise nofree","message":"LLVMBackend lowers Intrinsic::Malloc to an ordinary declaration of zyntax_alloc with no noalias return, allocsize or allockind. LLVM cannot recognize a fresh, potentially removable allocation. SOR's native execution sample on macOS spends substantial samples in zyntax_alloc, zyntax_free and pool allocation bookkeeping; Lua op_overload likewise allocates its loop-carried tables. This is a shared optimization opportunity, though benchmark gains need measurement.\n\nAlso, apply_internal_attributes marks every non-exported internal function nofree, even though its HIR can use Free/Realloc or call runtime functions that release earlier allocations. LLVM's nofree guarantee covers indirect deallocation too, so the blanket annotation is unjustified.\n\nDescribe fresh pool allocation results and size explicitly so LLVM's allocation analysis can eliminate nonescaping temporaries, while retaining allocator effects on memory (collection and host callbacks can observe memory). Remove unconditional nofree; leave LLVM to infer it for bodies that justify it. Do not describe the general zyntax_free as a standard free: it accepts shared static boxes and foreign allocations as well as pool blocks.\n\nValidation should show actual allocation elimination in optimized LLVM IR, verifier success and a release-taking internal function without nofree. Benchmark both frontends with normal tiering and LLVM enabled; keep allocator metadata only with correct behavior and document its measured effect.\n\nLLVM semantics: https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#function-attributes","files":null}]}